    "problem_statement": "Time parsing returns incorrect values for hour, minute, second components\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen parsing ISO 8601 time strings, the `parse_time` function returns time objects with swapped hour/minute/second values and incorrect microsecond calculations.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport isodate\n\n# Basic time parsing\nresult = isodate.parse_time(\"23:20:50\")\nprint(f\"Expected: hour=23, minute=20, second=50\")\nprint(f\"Got: hour={result.hour}, minute={result.minute}, second={result.second}\")\n\n# Time with microseconds\nresult = isodate.parse_time(\"15:33:42.123456\")\nprint(f\"Expected: hour=15, minute=33, second=42, microsecond=123456\")\nprint(f\"Got: hour={result.hour}, minute={result.minute}, second={result.second}, microsecond={result.microsecond}\")\n\n# Time with timezone\nresult = isodate.parse_time(\"15:27:46+01:00\")\nprint(f\"Expected: hour=15, minute=27, second=46\")\nprint(f\"Got: hour={result.hour}, minute={result.minute}, second={result.second}\")\n```\n\nThe function appears to be mixing up the order of time components and using incorrect decimal separator handling for fractional seconds."
